{"id":"ccl-earnings-fiscal-q3-2026","title":"CCL earnings — Carnival Corporation, fiscal Q3 2026","section":"Earnings","date":"2026-09-29","html":"<p><strong>This report is old: the quarter ended Aug 31, and it is about to be replaced by the December report, which brings the first full 2027 guide.</strong></p>\n<p>Management sees 2027 about half booked at record occupancy and price, and the one thing that could spoil it is marine fuel, because Carnival does not hedge.</p>\n<p>Reported 2026-09-29. Next report: December, date not found. Updated 2026-11-12. Research, not investment advice.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-short-version\">The short version</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Demand:</strong> Stronger. 2027 about half booked at record occupancy and price; customer deposits a Q3 record of $7.6B.</li><li><strong>Guidance:</strong> Raised slightly. Full-year adjusted EPS about $2.24, up $0.02. Q4 adjusted EPS about $0.20, under the $0.24 consensus.</li><li><strong>Biggest worry:</strong> Unhedged marine fuel. About $2.25B for the year; Q4 guided at $896 a metric ton.</li><li><strong>Chart:</strong> Leans up. A daily close under $27.56 would end that.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"the-numbers\">The numbers</h3>\n<ul><li>Revenue $8,435M against $8,153M a year ago and $8.36B expected. A beat.</li><li>GAAP EPS $1.40, adjusted EPS $1.43, flat on last year despite about $0.10 of fuel and currency drag. The beat is adjusted against adjusted: $1.43 against consensus of $1.36. GAAP $1.40 clears it too, so nothing is being mixed.</li><li>Fuel cost per ton was $826 against $607 a year ago.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"what-management-said-about-the-road-ahead\">What management said about the road ahead</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Next quarter:</strong> Q4 adjusted EPS about $0.20, net yields up about 1.7%, fuel $896 a metric ton, capacity down 0.1%.</li><li><strong>Full year:</strong> Net yields up about 2.3% in constant currency (raised), costs excluding fuel up about 2.2%, adjusted EBITDA about $7.14B.</li><li><strong>Bookings:</strong> 2027 about half booked, occupancy and price at records. 2028 has started ahead of a year ago on both.</li><li><strong>Q1 2027:</strong> Still carries damage from this spring's booking disruption, mainly long-haul and exotic itineraries. Management separates that quarter from the rest of 2027.</li><li><strong>Supply:</strong> 2027 capacity up only about 0.5%. Caribbean industry capacity growth is acknowledged as a pressure.</li><li><strong>Quote:</strong> &quot;We delivered another quarter of top and bottom-line records&quot; (Josh Weinstein, CEO, press release).</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"what-management-is-worried-about\">What management is worried about</h3>\n<ol><li><strong>Fuel, unhedged:</strong> about $2.25B for 2026; about $150M of extra cost absorbed this year.</li><li><strong>Q1 2027 weakness:</strong> from the spring booking disruption; no figure given.</li><li><strong>Loyalty-programme accounting:</strong> a 0.4-point yield headwind in 2027.</li><li><strong>Caribbean capacity:</strong> industry growth there; no figure given.</li><li><strong>EU emission-allowance costs:</strong> named with fuel; no figure given.</li><li>A full Risk Factors comparison: not found.</li></ol>\n<h3 id=\"the-big-worry-marine-fuel-both-ways\">The big worry: marine fuel, both ways</h3>\n<p><strong>What would ease it</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Calm in the Strait of Hormuz. Waller at the Fed says insurance and transfers there add $20 to $30 a barrel. Brent was about $92.5 on Nov 11, down from above $110 in late October.</li><li>Talks holding. Brent fell to $96.54 on Nov 9 on de-escalation hopes, and VLSFO supply improved in Fujairah (ENGINE).</li><li>Refineries returning. Chevron says about 10% of global refining is offline and expects margins to ease after the near term.</li><li>With no hedges, every dollar of a fall reaches Carnival at once.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>What would make it worse</strong></p>\n<ul><li>More incidents in the Strait. A tanker hit a mine there on Nov 11 and WTI closed near $87.</li><li>A slow fix. Exxon's CEO expects product-market normalisation to run well through next year.</li><li>Distillate staying scarce. On Sept 30 the 20-port average was $860.50 a ton for VLSFO and $1,522 for marine gasoil; Fujairah stocks were about half the pre-conflict level and China suspended most October fuel exports (ShipUniverse, Oct 1).</li><li>No supply response. None of Exxon, Chevron or Occidental is raising spending. Occidental plans on $50 WTI and hedges with collars, so even a producer is not betting the shock lasts.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>How Carnival is placed against it</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Most exposed of your cruise names. Royal Caribbean is hedged 58% for 2026 and 53% for 2027; Carnival is at zero.</li><li>It covered this year's roughly $150M of extra fuel with more than $150M of operating gains and still raised the guide by $0.02. That is thin cover.</li><li>The balance sheet is better than it was: debt $23.9B, down from $26.6B, a second investment-grade rating, no secured debt.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"money-plans\">Money plans</h3>\n<ul><li>Nearly $1.2B of buybacks year to date and $618M of dividends; about $2B combined expected for 2026.</li><li>Total debt $23.9B, down from $26.6B. A December Fed hike is priced, which matters for refinancing.</li><li>Q4 capital spending about $1.2B. The next new ship class arrives in 2029.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"people-and-governance\">People and governance</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>CEO:</strong> Josh Weinstein, since August 2022 (MarketScreener lists July 31, 2022). Not a founder; he was chief operations officer and followed Arnold Donald.</li><li><strong>Chair:</strong> Micky Arison, listed as Executive Chair, son of founder Ted Arison. Chair and CEO are split, but the chair is an executive from the founding family.</li><li><strong>CFO:</strong> David Bernstein, CFO since 2003, age 68 per MarketScreener. A long tenure with no successor named.</li><li><strong>Successor named:</strong> not found, for CEO or CFO.</li><li><strong>Control:</strong> The Arison family and trusts hold about 6.8% of combined voting power, with the Arison Group at 7.6% of Carnival Corporation (Panabee summary of the 2026 proxy, AI-generated; treat as approximate). Dual-class shares: not found.</li><li><strong>Structure change:</strong> The dual-listed structure is gone. Holders approved unification on Apr 17, 2026, and it completed on May 7, 2026: one company, Carnival Corporation Ltd, moved from Panama to Bermuda. Carnival plc became a subsidiary and left the London exchange.</li><li><strong>Recent changes or investigations (Nov 2025 to Nov 2026):</strong> no officer or director change in the two filings read; all 11 directors were re-elected. No investigation found.</li><li><strong>How much rests on one person:</strong> Less than at a founder-run firm. The founding family chairs it but holds a single-digit vote; the open question is the CFO's eventual handover.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"does-the-chart-agree\">Does the chart agree?</h3>\n<figure class=\"chart\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" data-ticker=\"CCL\" viewBox=\"0 0 760 400\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"CCL · daily · my levels (not yours) chart\" font-family=\"Arial, sans-serif\"><text x=\"8\" y=\"18\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#20242C\" class=\"c-ink\">CCL · daily · my levels (not yours)</text><line x1=\"8\" x2=\"610\" y1=\"374.0\" y2=\"374.0\" stroke=\"#E3DACB\" stroke-width=\"1\" class=\"c-grid\"/><line x1=\"8\" x2=\"610\" y1=\"289.0\" y2=\"289.0\" stroke=\"#E3DACB\" stroke-width=\"1\" class=\"c-grid\"/><line x1=\"8\" x2=\"610\" y1=\"204.0\" y2=\"204.0\" stroke=\"#E3DACB\" stroke-width=\"1\" class=\"c-grid\"/><line x1=\"8\" x2=\"610\" y1=\"119.0\" y2=\"119.0\" stroke=\"#E3DACB\" stroke-width=\"1\" class=\"c-grid\"/><line x1=\"8\" x2=\"610\" y1=\"34.0\" y2=\"34.0\" stroke=\"#E3DACB\" stroke-width=\"1\" class=\"c-grid\"/><line x1=\"10.3\" x2=\"10.3\" y1=\"210.0\" y2=\"221.9\" stroke=\"#A63A2E\" str